# Fictional EP Tracklist

## Track 1: The Wall Answers After the Hand Stops

**Story & Context:** At 140 BPM, Deniz begins with three dry plucks separated by large gaps. Mara places kick under the first attack and leaves the next membrane bloom completely unaccompanied. The snare arrives only after the second bloom, establishing Dry/Bloom Split as the core hook. Lena plays a damped sus2 shell, while Selin enters with one low breath-heavy fifth after the membrane tail clears. The chorus uses Sub Vacancy: the mono sub disappears during every strong resonator response, making the track feel heavier by removing electronic low end.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Empty tram-depot test room, one hand just lifted away from the yaybahar while a distant membrane still visibly vibrates, red calibration marks on white wall panels, wet rails beyond a glass door. Original sparse lyric fragments: “my hand already stopped / the wall answered later / count the distance / leave the low end empty.”

## Track 2: Spring Number Eight Crosses the Snare

**Story & Context:** At 142 BPM, Mara programs the EP’s sharpest halftime beat. Deniz bows a sustained interval whose membrane response gradually shifts across the snare position as spring tension changes. Half-Time Hinge becomes explicit: the same beat feels late, then centered, then forward without any tempo change. Emre adds rim strokes only after the resonator bloom. Midway through, Deniz physically damps one spring for a four-bar Spring Mute; the missing resonance makes the room feel suddenly smaller before full coupling returns.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Close-up of a numbered spring tagged 8 crossing the foreground while a snare waveform is drawn on a calibration sheet behind it, steel ruler, grease pencil, fluorescent reflections. Original sparse lyric fragments: “spring eight / cross the snare / same speed, new weight / the room moved first.”

## Track 3: Atlas of a Room with No Reverb

**Story & Context:** The closer runs at 138 BPM and opens with no drums. Lena plays isolated quartal electric-piano dyads while Deniz bows long yaybahar tones that activate both membranes at different intensities. Mara introduces kick and snare only after sixteen bars. Membrane Crossfade becomes the main structural device, with one resonator foregrounded physically rather than panned digitally. In the final minute, drums, piano, clarinet, and sub disappear. Deniz plucks one note. The close attack dies quickly, but the membranes continue to answer. The EP ends on the natural acoustic bloom with no effect tail added.

